"Problems from the Book | Mathematical Association of America (https://old.maa.org/press/maa-reviews/problems-from-the-book)\nciteturn608search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Titu Andreescu and Gabriel DospinescuPublisher: ... I was surprised to see that Straight from the Book is not listed in the Bibliography to this text.) ... Experienced problem-solvers with a substantial background in undergraduate mathematics will probably get quite a lot out of this book, but I don’t see it as having the same level of appeal as, say, Number Theory: Concepts and Problems, another book co-authored by Andreescu in the XYZ series.\n\n# Problems from the Book\n\nImage\n\n###### \n\nTitu Andreescu and Gabriel Dospinescu\n\nPublisher: \n\nXYZ Press\n\nPublication Date: \n\n2017\n\nNumber of Pages: \n\n571\n\nFormat: \n\nHardcover\n\nEdition: \n\n2\n\nPrice: \n\n69.95\n\nISBN: \n\n9780979926907\n\nCategory: \n\nProblem Book\n\n  * MAA Review\n  * Table of Contents\n\n[Reviewed by\n\nMark Hunacek\n\n, on\n\n10/31/2017\n\n]\n\nThis is one of roughly two dozen Olympiad preparation problem books published by XYZ Press and distributed by the AMS. XYZ Press is a publishing company established by Andreescu, the co-author of this book and many others published under that imprint. The title of this book, though apparently not explained in the book itself, is obviously a nod to the famous dictum of Paul Erdős that God maintains The Book, in which are located the best and most elegant proofs of mathematical results. (See, for example, Proofs from THE BOOK, by Aigner and Ziegler.)\n\nThis entry in the XYZ series is divided into 23 chapters, each one averaging about 25–30 pages. Chapter topics range from individual results (e.g., the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality and the law of quadratic reciprocity) to general topics (e.g., a chapter entitled “primes and squares” and another on the use of complex numbers to solve combinatorial problems). Number theory, algebra and combinatorics are very heavily represented among the various problems; there are also some problems in analysis, but geometry problems are largely omitted. (I did notice one or two of them sneaking in and winding up getting solved by algebraic means.)\n\nEach chapter follows the same general pattern. First, necessary background results are stated and sometimes proved (a proof is provided, for example, of the law of quadratic reciprocity). Then there are roughly a dozen solved problems, followed by a lengthy selection of “Practice Problems”, solutions to which are not provided. (However, another XYZ book by the authors, Straight from the Book, was published in 2012; although unseen by me, its description states that it contains, among other things, solutions to the first 12 chapters of the first edition of the book now under review. I was surprised to see that Straight from the Book is not listed in the Bibliography to this text.)\n\nObviously, judging the quality or elegance of a mathematical problem is a highly subjective matter of individual taste. Perhaps because of this, or because I have seen a lot of problem books lately, or because I am not involved in preparing students for competitions and am more interested in material that I can use in lectures in my upper-level courses, or perhaps because the reference to The Book raised my expectations, I found myself a little underwhelmed at this collection of problems.\n\nTo me, a “book result” should be a short, snappy, memorable piece of mathematics with a particularly elegant proof. Many of the problems in this book struck me as rather technical results that make good problem-solving challenges but did not strike me as particularly memorable or of intrinsic interest in themselves — proving inequalities or identities, for example, that are so complicated that they cannot be comfortably reproduced here, or proving that some horrible-looking number is an integer, is irrational, or has some other property.  A week from now, I doubt I will be able to remember the results of most of the problems that I read in this text.\n\nThe solutions given for the examples in the book are often clever, yet, for the most part, very difficult, and often consist of fairly involved or lengthy calculations. It should definitely be stressed that this is not a book for neophytes. Experienced problem-solvers with a substantial background in undergraduate mathematics will probably get quite a lot out of this book, but I don’t see it as having the same level of appeal as, say, Number Theory: Concepts and Problems, another book co-authored by Andreescu in the XYZ series.\n\n* * *\n\nMark Hunacek (mhunacek@iastate.edu) teaches mathematics at Iowa State University. \n\nThe table of contents is not available.\n\nTags: \n\nProblems\n\nProblems Olympiad Level\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSolutions to Problems from the Book | PDF | Field (Mathematics) | Combinatorics (https://www.scribd.com/document/671331832/1)\nciteturn608search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 3.1 years ago; Overall, the book presents problem solutions while using them as a jumping off point to introduce cutting-edge mathematical concepts and their applications. ... # Straight from the Book ... Gabriel Dospinescu ... Titu Andreescu Gabriel Dospinesca\n\n0 ratings 0% found this document useful (0 votes)\n\n241 views 50 pages\n\n# \n\nSolutions to Problems from the Book\n\nThis book provides solutions to problems presented in the first 12 chapters of another book, Problems from the Book. It begins with solutions to problems involving topics like Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, algebraic number theory, and formal power series. Each solution is accompanied by explanations of different solution methods and the mathematics involved. The book also includes extensive discussions of modern mathematical topics that provide context for the solutions. It aims to balance problem-solving with presenting professional mathematics. Overall, the book presents problem solutions while using them as a jumping off point to introduce cutting-edge mathematical concepts and their applications.\n\n## Uploaded by\n\nUniversität Bielefeld\n\nAvailable Formats\n\nDownload as PDF, TXT or read online on Scribd\n\nPage view\n\nDownload\n\n# Straight from the Book\n\nTitu Andreescu\nGabriel Dospinescu\n\n## Straight from the Book\n\nPress\nTitu Andreescu Gabriel Dospinesca\nUniversity of Texas at Dallas Ecole Normale Supérieure, Lyon\n\n## The only way to learn mathematics is to do mathematics.\n\n## Library of Congress Control Number: 2012951362\n\n~Paul Halmos\nISBN-13: 978-0-9799269-3-8 ISBN-10: 0-9799269-3-9\n\n## © 2012 XYZ Press, LLC\n\nForeword\n\nThis book is a follow-on from the authors’ earlier book, ‘Problems from\nthe Book’. However, it can certainly be read as a stand-alone book: it is not\nvital to have read the earlier book.\nThe previous book was based around a collection of problems. In contrast,\nthis book is based around a collection of solutions. These are solutions to\nsome of the (often extremely challenging) problems from the earlier book.\nThe topics chosen reflect those from the first twelve chapters of the previous\nbook: s0 we have Cauchy-Schwarz, Algebraic Number Theory, Formal Series,\nLagrange Interpolation, to name but a few.\nThe book is one of the most remarkable mathematical texts I have ever\nseen. First of all, there is the richness of the problems, and the huge variety\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStraight from the Book - Titu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu (https://carturesti.ro/carte/straight-from-the-book-599930426)\nciteturn608search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; Straight from the Book Titu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu ... Publisher: ... Provides solutions to the problems which were originally proposed in the first 12 chapters of Problems from the Book.\n\n„Straight from the Book”  în librăriile Cărturești\n\nExistă posibilitatea ca stocurile să varieze. Te invităm să ai un plan de rezervă pentru situațiile în care produsul dorit este de negăsit la raft sau a fost vândut.\n\nThis product is available only for online order.\n\nPuțintică răbdare...\n\nWhere to find us\n\nStraight from the Book Titu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu\n\n# Straight from the Book\n\nTitu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu\n\nCategories:\n\nBooks in Foreign Languages, General History\n\nPublishing Date:\n\n2012\n\nPublisher:\n\nXYZ Press\n\nCover Type:\n\nHardcover\n\nPage Count:\n\n590\n\nISBN:\n\n9780979926938\n\nDimensions: l: 21.8cm | H: 17.9cm | 4cm | 1044g\n\nUnavailable\n\nPrice applicable only to online purchases!\n\nFree Gift Wrapping!\n\nFree shipping over 150 RON\n\nYou can return it in 14 days\n\nYou got questions? Contact Us!\n\nPublisher's Synopsis\n\nProvides solutions to the problems which were originally proposed in the first 12 chapters of Problems from the Book. The solutions are used as motivation for the introduction of some very clear mathematical expositions. This is absolutely state-of-the-art material. Everyone who loves mathematics and mathematical thinking should read this book.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProblems from the Book, by Titu Andreescu and Gabriel Dospinescu. Pp. 554. $69-95. 2008. ISBN 978-0-9799269-0-7 (XYZ Press). | The Mathematical Gazette | Cambridge Core (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/mathematical-gazette/article/abs/problems-from-the-book-by-titu-andreescu-and-gabriel-dospinescu-pp-554-6995-2008-isbn-9780979926907-xyz-press/CE76A6A884EBA437BD2160C7A40BBFCE)\nciteturn608search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Problems from the Book, by Titu Andreescu and Gabriel Dospinescu.\n\n# Problems from the Book, by Titu Andreescu and Gabriel Dospinescu. Pp. 554. $69-95. 2008. ISBN 978-0-9799269-0-7 (XYZ Press).\n\n## Review products\n\nProblems from the Book, by Titu Andreescu and Gabriel Dospinescu. Pp. 554. $69-95. 2008. ISBN 978-0-9799269-0-7 (XYZ Press).\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  23 January 2015\n\nImre Leader\n\nShow author details\n\n* * *\n\nImre Leader*\n    Affiliation:\n\n## Abstract\n\nAn abstract is not available for this content so a preview has been provided. Please use the Get access link above for information on how to access this content.\n\nImage: Image of the first page of this content. For PDF version, please use the ‘Save PDF’ preceeding this image.'\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Reviews\n\nInformation\n    \n\nThe Mathematical Gazette , Volume 94 , Issue 530 , July 2010 , pp. 380 - 381\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025557200007038 [Opens in a new window]\n\nProblems from the Book, by Titu Andreescu and Gabriel Dospinescu. Pp. 554. $69-95. 2008. ISBN 978-0-9799269-0-7 (XYZ Press).\n\n  * Volume 94, Issue 530\n  * Imre Leader ^{(a1)}\n  * DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025557200007038\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProblems from the Book by Andreescu, Titu, Dospinescu, Gabriel (9780979926907) (https://www.allbookstores.com/Problems-Book-Andreescu-Titu-Dospinescu/9780979926907)\nciteturn608search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; Publisher: ... I really appreciate the authors rarely repeat techniques, topics and problems stated in other popular problem-solving books such as Art and Craft of Problem-Solving or Problem Solving Strategies. ... I would give 4 stars for this, if it wasn't for the fact that another book was recently published (Straight from the Book), that includes the solutions to all the problems. ... The title of this book is Problems from the Book and it was written by Titu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu, Andreescu, Titu.\nIn this volume they present innumerable beautiful results, intriguing problems, and ingenious solutions. The problems range from elementary gems to deep truths. A trully delightful and highly instructive book, this will prepare the engaged reader not only for any mathematics competition they may enter but also for a life time of mathematical enjoyment. A must for the bookshelves of both aspiring and seasoned mathematicians.\n\n### Customer Book Reviews\n\nRated 4.8/5 based on 16 customer reviews\n\nProblems from the Book\n\nA valuable resource... for the right reader\n\nBy Daniel Finkel on Oct 17, 2013\n\nWhen a math book uses the phrase \"from the book,\" in the title, you know they're being ambitious. The reference is to Erdos's description of wanting to experience the mathematics in \"God's Book,\" of all time beautiful problems. And in large part, the authors live up to their ambitions. This book is a collection of excellent, deep problems, along with how to attack them. Be warned, though, that the barrier to entry is extremely high. I really appreciate the authors rarely repeat techniques, topics and problems stated in other popular problem-solving books such as Art and Craft of Problem-Solving or Problem Solving Strategies. As such, if you are new to problem-solving, you should definitely start with something else, but if it's something you've been doing for a while, this is, in my opinion, the best next step to take. The proofs are always explained very clearly. While following some the solutions usually does require pen & paper to make your own notes, I never felt I had to be \"solving the solution\" as I sometimes am in other books and resources. There are 23 chapters in total on algebra, number theory and combinatorics (conveniently, they are arranged approximately according to difficulty). Each begins with some theory and then follows examples with solutions (usually around 10). At the end of each chapter is a list of many problems that all use the topics previously elaborated. These problems do not have solutions - while that is unerstandable, it is a sad fact that there are no hints for tackling them. I would give 4 stars for this, if it wasn't for the fact that another book was recently published (Straight from the Book), that includes the solutions to all the problems. By now, Problems from the Book is one of the most well-established books, so I had high expectations, yet it manages to live up to its name.\n\nAs such, if you are new to problem-solving, you should definitely start with something else, but if it's something you've been doing for a while, this is, in my opinion, the best next step to take. The proofs are always explained very clearly. While following some the solutions usually does require pen & paper to make your own notes, I never felt I had to be \"solving the solution\" as I sometimes am in other books and resources. There are 23 chapters in total on algebra, number theory and combinatorics (conveniently, they are arranged approximately according to difficulty). Each begins with some theory and then follows examples with solutions (usually around 10). At the end of each chapter is a list of many problems that all use the topics previously elaborated. These problems do not have solutions - while that is unerstandable, it is a sad fact that there are no hints for tackling them. I would give 4 stars for this, if it wasn't for the fact that another book was recently published (Straight from the Book), that includes the solutions to all the problems. By now, Problems from the Book is one of the most well-established books, so I had high expectations, yet it manages to live up to its name.\n\n4.0 out of 5 stars\n\nBy Daniel Finkel on Oct 17, 2013\n\nWhen a math book uses the phrase \"from the book,\" in the title, you know they're being ambitious. The reference is to Erdos's description of wanting to experience the mathematics in \"God's Book,\" of all time beautiful problems. And in large part, the authors live up to their ambitions. This book is a collection of excellent, deep problems, along with how to attack them. Be warned, though, that the barrier to entry is extremely high. A remarkably bright math student I know said that this was the first book recommended to him when he became interested in math contests... at the age of 10. The experience of trying to read it proved very discouraging for him. I can imagine no 10-year-old, no matter how brilliant, who would benefit from reading this book. The appropriate audience is probably 1 in a million high school juniors or seniors, and 1 in 50 college mathematics majors. Basically, if you can get a problem right on the Putnam and you want to take it to the next level, then this book may be for you. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStraight from the Book by Titu Andreescu | Goodreads (https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/30979625-straight-from-the-book)\nciteturn608search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Straight from the Book ... Titu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu ... Its main objective is to provide solutions to the problems which were originally proposed in the first 12 chapters of \"Problems from the Book\". ... He has also authored a large number of books on the topic of problem solving and Olympiad-style mathematics.\n\n# Straight from the Book\n\n### \n\nTitu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu\n\n4.33 3 ratings1 review\n\nWant to Read\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\nThis book is a compilation of many suggestions, much advice, and even more hard work. Its main objective is to provide solutions to the problems which were originally proposed in the first 12 chapters of \"Problems from the Book\". The volume is far more than a collection of solutions. The solutions are used as motivation for the introduction of some very clear expositions of mathematics. And this is modern, current, up-to-the-minute mathematics. This is absolutely state-of-the-art material. Everyone who loves mathematics and mathematical thinking should acquire this book.\n\nGenresMathematics\n\n590 pages, Hardcover\n\nPublished August 31, 2012\n\n### About the author\n\n#### Titu Andreescu\n\n83 books 50 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nTitu Andreescu is an associate professor of mathematics at the University of Texas at Dallas. He is also firmly involved in mathematics contests and Olympiads, being the Director of AMC (as appointed by the Mathematical Association of America), Director of MOP, Head Coach of the USA IMO Team and Chairman of the USAMO. He has also authored a large number of books on the topic of problem solving and Olympiad-style mathematics.\n\n## Ratings & Reviews\n\nWhat do you think?\n\nWrite a Review\n\n* * *\n\n### Friends & Following\n\nCreate a free account to discover what your friends think of this book!\n\n### Community Reviews\n\n4.33\n\n3 ratings 1 review\n\n5 stars\n\n1 (33%)\n\n4 stars\n\n2 (66%)\n\n3 stars\n\n0 (0%)\n\n2 stars\n\n0 (0%)\n\n1 star\n\n0 (0%)\n\nSearch review text\n\n[Input: Search review text]\n\nFilters\n\nDisplaying 1 - 1 of 1 review\n\nJuan Belza\n\n34 reviews 1 follower\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nMarch 18, 2023\n\nGood book. Can be a little unfriendly for people unaware of certain notation and topics. Though I understand this is supposed to be a challenging book, I still think it's better to make everything more clear, an example of this being Evan Chen's EGMO. Still, very good ideas and recurring patterns in olympiad problems are brought to light, and some solutions were extremely clever.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStraight From the Book -- Andreescu, Titu; Dospinescu, Gabriel -- XYZ Series; 6, 1, 2012 -- XYZ Press -- 9780979926938 -- 29b65c5a7abf56710f068b1c1b23c8fc -- Anna’s Archive | PDF (https://www.scribd.com/document/1076135121/Straight-From-the-Book-Andreescu-Titu-Dospinescu-Gabriel-XYZ-Series-6-1-2012-XYZ-Press-9780979926938-29b65c5a7abf56710f068b1c1b23)\nciteturn608search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Straight From the Book -- Andreescu, Titu; Dospinescu, Gabriel -- XYZ Series; 6, 1, 2012 -- XYZ Press -- 9780979926938 -- 29b65c5a7abf56710f068b1c1b23c8fc -- Anna’s Archive ... Titu Andreescu Gabriel Dospinescu Straight from the Book XYZ Press Titu Andreescu Gabriel Dospinescu University of Texas at Dallas Ecole Normale Supérieure, Lyon Library of Congress Control Number: 2012951362 ISBN-13: 978-0-9799269-3-8 ISBN-10: 0-9799269-3-9 © 2012 XYZ Press, LLC All rights reserved. ... Although we never underestimated the role of problem solving, we strongly believe that the reader will benefit more from embarking on a mathematical journey rather than navigating a huge list of scattered problems.\n We were not able to provide full solutions in our first volume due to the lack of space. In addition, the statements of the proposed problems contained typos and some elementary mistakes which needed further editing. Finally, the problems were also considered to be quite difficult to tackle. With these points in mind, we came up with a two-part plan: to correct the identified errors and to publish comprehensive solutions to the problems. The first task, editing the statements of the proposed problems, was sim- ple and has already been completed in the second edition of Problems from the Book. Although we focused on changing several problems, we also introduced many new ones. The second task, providing full solutions, however, proved to be more challenging than expected, so we asked for help. We created a forum on [Link] (a familiar site for problem-solving enthusiasts) where solutions to the proposed problems were gathered. It was a great pleasure to witness the passion with which some of the best problem-solvers on mathlinks attacked these tough-nuts. This new book is the result of their common effort, and we thank them. Providing solutions to every problem within the limited space of one vol- ume turned out to be an optimistic plan. Only the solutions to problems from the first 12 chapters of the second edition of Problems from the Book are presented here. Furthermore, many of the problems are difficult and require a rather extensive mathematical background. We decided, therefore, to com- plement the problems and solutions with a series of addenda, using various problems as starting points for excursions into ”real mathematics”. Although we never underestimated the role of problem solving, we strongly believe that the reader will benefit more from embarking on a mathematical journey rather than navigating a huge list of scattered problems. This book tries to reconcile problem-solving with ” professional mathematics”. Let us now delve into the structure of the book which consists of 12 chap- ters and presents the problems and solutions proposed in the corresponding chapters of Problems from the Book, second edition. Many of these problems are fairly difficult and different approaches are presented for a majority of them. At the end of each chapter, we acknowledge those who provided so- lutions. Some chapters are followed by one or two addenda, which present topics of more advanced mathematics stemming from the elementary topics discussed in the problems of that chapter. The first two chapters focus on elementary algebraic inequalities (a no- table caveat is that some of the problems are quite challenging) and there is not much to comment regarding these chapters except for the fact that al- gebraic inequalities have proven fairly popular at mathematics competitions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStraight from the Book (https://xyzpressbeta.weebly.com/store/p2/Straight_from_the_Book.html)\nciteturn608search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; ## Straight from the Book ... By Titu Andreescu and Gabriel Dospinescu ...   * Publisher: XYZ Press (2012)\n\n## Straight from the Book\n\nSKU:\n\n$69.95\n\n$69.95\n\nUnavailable\n\nper item\n\nBy Titu Andreescu and Gabriel Dospinescu\n\nThis book is a compilation of many suggestions, much advice, and even more hard work. Its main objective is to provide solutions to the problems which were originally proposed in the first 12 chapters of \"Problems from the Book\". The volume is far more than a collection of solutions. The solutions are used as motivation for the introduction of some very clear expositions of mathematics. And this is modern, current, up-to-the-minute mathematics. This is absolutely state-of-the-art material. Everyone who loves mathematics and mathematical thinking should acquire this book.\n\nProduct Details\n\n  * Hardcover: 590 pages\n  * Publisher: XYZ Press (2012)\n  * Language: English\n  * ISBN-10: 0979926939\n  * ISBN-13: 978-0979926938\n  * Product Dimensions: 8.5 x 7 x 1.8 inches--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGabriel Dospinescu (Author of Problems from the Book) (https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/4856070.Gabriel_Dospinescu)\nciteturn608search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;  | Problems from the Bookby Titu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu ...  | Straight from the Book\n | Number Theory: Concepts and Problems (Xyz Series)\nby Titu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu, Oleg Mushkarov\n4.50 avg rating — 4 ratings  | [Input][Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] Want to Read saving… [Input][Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] Rate this book Clear rating 1 of 5 stars2 of 5 stars3 of 5 stars4 of 5 stars5 of 5 stars\n | Straight from the Book\nby Titu Andreescu, Gabriel Dospinescu\n4.33 avg rating — 3 ratings  | [Input][Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] Want to Read saving… [Input][Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] Rate this book Clear rating 1 of 5 stars2 of 5 stars3 of 5 stars4 of 5 stars5 of 5 stars\n | Autour de la correspondance de Langlands locale p-adique